Fixed — and it wasn't your packages, your network, or permissions.

**What was actually wrong.** Your system Python (`/usr/local/bin/python3`, 3.13.1) had pip's *metadata* but not pip's *code*:

```
/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/
  README.txt
  pip-24.3.1.dist-info/    ← claims pip is installed
                            ← no pip/ directory beside it
```

So every entry point died with `/usr/local/bin/python3: No module named pip`, including the `/usr/local/bin/pip3` wrapper. That shape — `dist-info` present, package tree gone — is what an interrupted upgrade or uninstall leaves behind.

I ruled the plausible alternatives out rather than assuming: no PEP 668 gate on this interpreter (the `EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` marker exists only for the distro 3.11 under `/usr/lib/python3.11/`), site-packages was writable, no `pip.conf` or `PIP_*` overrides, and a throwaway venv installed `six` from PyPI fine — so the index and network were never the problem.

**The repair.** Moved the stale metadata aside and reinstalled pip from the wheel Python already ships, no network bootstrap:

```
mv .../site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info /tmp/pip-24.3.1.dist-info.stale-bak
python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade --default-pip
```

**Proof it works**, not just that it imports — a real download and install:

```
$ python3 -m pip install --no-input six
Successfully installed six-1.17.0
$ python3 -m pip --version
pip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)
$ python3 -m pip check
No broken requirements found.
```

I checked this the hard way deliberately: my first verification was `pip install --dry-run pip`, which came back "Requirement already satisfied" and proved nothing, since it resolves locally without touching a download.

Written up in `docs/pip-repair.md`, including the ruled-out hypotheses table and a one-liner that spots this failure mode again in a single call.

Two things worth knowing:
- `/tmp/pip-24.3.1.dist-info.stale-bak` is the old metadata, kept for recovery — safe to delete. `six` 1.17.0 is now installed system-wide as the proof; remove it if you don't want it.
- Your distro Python 3.11 at `/usr/bin/python3.11` was untouched, and its pip is still absent — that was already true and I left it alone.

For project work, use a venv rather than system installs; this interpreter has no PEP 668 guard, so pip will happily let you modify the system site-packages.

